## Coding Rules
1. TypeScript strict mode — no `any` types unless unavoidable
2. Tests live next to source files (*.test.ts / *.test.tsx)
3. Run `npm test` before committing
4. All AI API calls go through OpenRouter
5. API routes support SSE (Server-Sent Events) for streaming (?stream=true for chat)
6. Chat errors return 503 + queued status when exchanges unreachable
7. Dashboard uses visibility-aware polling (pauses when tab hidden)
8. Rate limiting is distributed-ready (Redis fallback to in-memory)
9. Environment variables in .env.local (never commit secrets)
10. Follow existing code patterns — check similar files first

## Extension Protocol (BlackBox AI)
BlackBox extension communicates with FreedomForge via HTTP polling (no WebSocket):
- **Health endpoint:** GET /api/status/extension (returns agent_state, models_available, circuit_breakers)
- **Chat endpoint:** POST /api/chat (supports ?stream=true for SSE)
- **Queue sync:** POST /api/sync/queue (triggered by Service Worker on reconnect)
- **Polling frequency:** 5-30s recommended (register at /api/status/extension to discover)
- **Auth:** Session cookie (ff_session) required in all requests
- **Heartbeat:** /api/health every 30s; if 503 → circuit breaker is OPEN
- **Exponential backoff:** On connection loss, retry with 2s, 4s, 8s... max 60s jitter

## When Generating Code
- Use React Server Components where possible
- Use 'use client' directive only when needed (state, effects, browser APIs)
- Prefer async/await over .then() chains
- Use the circuit breaker pattern for external API calls
- Follow the existing error handling patterns
- Keep security in mind — validate inputs, sanitize outputs
- Add health checks before all mutations (trades, payouts, etc.)
- Queue mutations if external services are unreachable (don't fail silently)

